Lab Tasks
Task 1: Subscribe to the OpenShift Service Mesh Operator
OpenShift Service Mesh is based on the open-source Istio project and provides a comprehensive service mesh solution. The first step is to install the required operators.

Subtask 1.1: Verify Cluster Access
First, let's verify that you have proper access to the OpenShift cluster.

# Check cluster connection
oc whoami

# Verify cluster version
oc version

# Check if you have cluster-admin privileges
oc auth can-i '*' '*'
Subtask 1.2: Create Required Namespaces
OpenShift Service Mesh requires specific namespaces for its operators.

# Create the openshift-operators-redhat namespace
oc create namespace openshift-operators-redhat

# Create the openshift-distributed-tracing namespace
oc create namespace openshift-distributed-tracing
Subtask 1.3: Install Jaeger Operator
The Jaeger operator is required for distributed tracing capabilities.

# Create Jaeger operator subscription
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: jaeger-product
  namespace: openshift-distributed-tracing
spec:
  channel: stable
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
  name: jaeger-product
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
EOF
Subtask 1.4: Install Kiali Operator
Kiali provides observability and management capabilities for the service mesh.

# Create Kiali operator subscription
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: kiali-ossm
  namespace: openshift-operators
spec:
  channel: stable
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
  name: kiali-ossm
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
EOF
Subtask 1.5: Install OpenShift Service Mesh Operator
Now install the main Service Mesh operator.

# Create Service Mesh operator subscription
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: servicemeshoperator
  namespace: openshift-operators
spec:
  channel: stable
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
  name: servicemeshoperator
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
EOF
Subtask 1.6: Verify Operator Installation
Wait for all operators to be installed successfully.

# Check Jaeger operator status
oc get csv -n openshift-distributed-tracing

# Check Kiali operator status
oc get csv -n openshift-operators | grep kiali

# Check Service Mesh operator status
oc get csv -n openshift-operators | grep servicemesh

# Wait for all operators to show "Succeeded" phase
oc get csv -n openshift-operators -w
Task 2: Create the Service Mesh Control Plane (SMCP)
The Service Mesh Control Plane manages the service mesh infrastructure and configuration.

Subtask 2.1: Create Control Plane Namespace
Create a dedicated namespace for the service mesh control plane.

# Create istio-system namespace
oc create namespace istio-system
Subtask 2.2: Create Service Mesh Control Plane Configuration
Create a comprehensive SMCP configuration with essential features enabled.

# Create the Service Mesh Control Plane
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: maistra.io/v2
kind: ServiceMeshControlPlane
metadata:
  name: basic
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  version: v2.4
  tracing:
    type: Jaeger
    sampling: 10000
  addons:
    jaeger:
      name: jaeger
      install:
        storage:
          type: Memory
    kiali:
      name: kiali
      enabled: true
      install:
        dashboard:
          viewOnly: false
    grafana:
      name: grafana
      enabled: true
  policy:
    type: Istiod
  telemetry:
    type: Istiod
  security:
    dataPlane:
      mtls: true
    controlPlane:
      mtls: true
  gateways:
    ingress:
      enabled: true
      runtime:
        deployment:
          replicas: 2
    egress:
      enabled: true
      runtime:
        deployment:
          replicas: 2
  runtime:
    components:
      pilot:
        deployment:
          replicas: 2
        container:
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 100m
              memory: 128Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 500m
              memory: 512Mi
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Monitor Control Plane Installation
The control plane installation takes several minutes. Monitor the progress.

# Watch the SMCP status
oc get smcp -n istio-system -w

# Check detailed status
oc describe smcp basic -n istio-system

# Monitor pod creation
oc get pods -n istio-system -w
Subtask 2.4: Create Service Mesh Member Roll
Create a ServiceMeshMemberRoll to define which namespaces are part of the mesh.

# Create a sample application namespace
oc create namespace bookinfo

# Create Service Mesh Member Roll
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: maistra.io/v1
kind: ServiceMeshMemberRoll
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  members:
  - bookinfo
EOF
Task 3: Verify Installation Status
Comprehensive verification ensures all components are working correctly.

Subtask 3.1: Check Control Plane Status
Verify that the Service Mesh Control Plane is fully operational.

# Check SMCP status - should show "ComponentsReady"
oc get smcp basic -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Ready")].status}'

# Get detailed SMCP information
oc get smcp basic -n istio-system -o yaml

# Verify all control plane pods are running
oc get pods -n istio-system
Expected output should show pods like:

istiod-basic-xxx (Pilot)
istio-proxy (in each pod)
jaeger-xxx
kiali-xxx
grafana-xxx
Subtask 3.2: Verify Operator Health
Check that all operators are healthy and functioning.

# Check operator pod status
oc get pods -n openshift-operators | grep -E "(servicemesh|kiali)"
oc get pods -n openshift-distributed-tracing | grep jaeger

# Verify operator logs for any errors
oc logs -n openshift-operators deployment/istio-operator
Subtask 3.3: Test Service Mesh Components
Verify that key service mesh components are accessible.

# Check if Kiali is accessible
oc get route kiali -n istio-system

# Check if Jaeger is accessible
oc get route jaeger -n istio-system

# Check if Grafana is accessible
oc get route grafana -n istio-system

# Test service mesh injection capability
oc get mutatingwebhookconfiguration | grep istio
Subtask 3.4: Verify Member Roll Status
Ensure the member roll is properly configured.

# Check ServiceMeshMemberRoll status
oc get smmr default -n istio-system -o yaml

# Verify namespace is properly configured for mesh
oc get namespace bookinfo -o yaml | grep -A 5 labels
Subtask 3.5: Deploy Test Application
Deploy a simple test application to verify mesh functionality.

# Switch to bookinfo namespace
oc project bookinfo

# Deploy a test pod with sidecar injection
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: test-app
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: test-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: test-app
      annotations:
        sidecar.istio.io/inject: "true"
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: test-app
        image: registry.redhat.io/ubi8/ubi:latest
        command: ["/bin/sleep", "3600"]
EOF
Subtask 3.6: Verify Sidecar Injection
Check that the Istio sidecar proxy is automatically injected.

# Wait for deployment to be ready
oc rollout status deployment/test-app -n bookinfo

# Check that pod has 2 containers (app + sidecar)
oc get pods -n bookinfo -l app=test-app

# Verify sidecar injection
oc describe pod -n bookinfo -l app=test-app | grep -A 10 "istio-proxy"

# Check sidecar configuration
oc get pod -n bookinfo -l app=test-app -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[*].name}'
Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
Issue 1: Operators not installing

# Check operator catalog source
oc get catalogsource -n openshift-marketplace

# Verify subscription status
oc get subscription -A | grep -E "(servicemesh|kiali|jaeger)"

# Check install plans
oc get installplan -A
Issue 2: Control Plane not ready

# Check SMCP events
oc get events -n istio-system --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'

# Verify resource requirements
oc describe nodes | grep -A 5 "Allocated resources"

# Check for resource constraints
oc get limitrange -A
Issue 3: Sidecar injection not working

# Verify webhook configuration
oc get mutatingwebhookconfiguration istio-sidecar-injector -o yaml

# Check namespace labels
oc get namespace bookinfo --show-labels

# Verify SMMR membership
oc get smmr default -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.status.members}'
